\section{Threat Model and Adversarial Analysis}
\label{sec:threatmodel}

The cryptosystem is designed to resist both classical and quantum-enabled
adversaries. The following threat classes are explicitly modeled:

\subsection{Quantum Adversaries}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Shor-based factorization and discrete log attacks.
    \item Grover-accelerated search over keyspace.
    \item Quantum oracle amplification against ciphertext structure.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Side-Channel Adversaries}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Electromagnetic leakage profiling.
    \item Thermal dissipation pattern extraction.
    \item Acoustic resonance correlation.
    \item Timing jitter inference.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Fault Injection Adversaries}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Voltage glitching,
    \item Clock skew injection,
    \item Laser fault induction,
    \item Rowhammer-style memory corruption.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cryptanalytic Adversaries}

\begin{itemize}
    \item LWE parameter reduction,
    \item Gaussian noise estimation,
    \item ciphertext malleability probing,
    \item homomorphic gate misuse.
\end{itemize}

The system’s layered architecture ensures that compromise of any single
component does not expose plaintext or private key material.